"{\n  \"test\": {\n    \"name\": \"VERBAL ABILITY _RC 02\",\n    \"testTotalDuration\": 1200000,\n    \"settings\": {\n      \"fonts\": {},\n      \"isSectionSwitchingEnabled\": true\n    },\n    \"practiceTestUrls\": [],\n    \"testFormat\": \"DEFAULT\"\n  },\n  \"filters\": {\n    \"incorrect\": 1,\n    \"correct\": 0,\n    \"partiallyMarked\": 0,\n    \"unattempted\": 1,\n    \"markForReview\": 0,\n    \"graceMarked\": 0,\n    \"revaluated\": 0\n  },\n  \"timeTaken\": 188000,\n  \"courseId\": \"844567\",\n  \"showReportQuestion\": true,\n  \"sectionWiseStats\": [\n    {\n      \"marksScored\": -0.25,\n      \"sectionMarks\": 15,\n      \"accuracy\": 0,\n      \"_id\": \"6ab028d104af86206e33834d\",\n      \"totalQuestions\": 15,\n      \"attemptDuration\": 188000,\n      \"correctAnswers\": 0,\n      \"inCorrectAnswers\": 1,\n      \"partiallyCorrectAnswers\": 0,\n      \"unAttempted\": 14,\n      \"name\": \"VERBAL ABOLITY TEST 01\",\n      \"order\": 1,\n      \"sectionDuration\": 0,\n      \"sectionId\": \"60f69fb8e70c56aee9070d22\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"sections\": [\n    {\n      \"draftMode\": false,\n      \"isActive\": true,\n      \"sectionDuration\": 0,\n      \"sectionMarks\": 15,\n      \"sectionInstructions\": \"<p>PLEASE ATTEMPT EVERY QUESTION</p>\",\n      \"hasOptionalQuestions\": false,\n      \"_id\": \"60f69fb8e70c56aee9070d22\",\n      \"name\": \"VERBAL ABOLITY TEST 01\",\n      \"order\": 1,\n      \"createdAt\": \"2024-04-04T10:11:29.326Z\",\n      \"updatedAt\": \"2024-04-04T10:11:29.326Z\",\n      \"questions\": [\n        {\n          \"_id\": \"60f69fd0104d43d98b76b0ac\",\n          \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Directions (1-5):&nbsp;Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">For almost two decades, the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) has been reporting on the increasing number of over-exploited blocks across India, the &lsquo;dark&rsquo; category blocks. The recent annual book of CGWB has reported 1,034 units, out of the 6,584 units it monitors, as over-exploited. If this is the &lsquo;crisis&rsquo;, then we have had it for long. What has this not received enough attention? Is it because these zones are not in cities?</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Just to be sure how critical the &lsquo;crisis&rsquo; is, CGWB&rsquo;s 2013 estimates say that the groundwater development in India is just about 62% of the utilisable groundwater reserves. Similarly, a recent report by the Central Water Commission, prepared in collaboration with the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), asserted that India is not yet in &ldquo;water scarcity condition&rdquo;. But it is certainly in a &ldquo;water-stressed condition&rdquo;, with reducing per capita water availability. Here, we certainly don&rsquo;t mean to say that India can continue with the present ways of water management. We also cannot remain in a state of denial that a crisis is not in the making. However, certain steps need to be taken to ensure a more useful and productive discourse about water governance challenges.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">First, India needs to reconsider the institutional processes for dissemination of knowledge about water resource management. There is a certain amount of danger inherent in the casual manner in which knowledge about water resources is legitimised and consumed, particularly in these days of &lsquo;viral&rsquo; information.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /></span></strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Second,&nbsp;<em>we need to recognise the crisis is not as much of scarcity as of delivery.</em>&nbsp;The challenge is to ensure an adequate access to quality water, more so in urban areas where inequities over space and time are acute. We need to also realise that with the country&rsquo;s rapid urbanisation, demand cannot be met by groundwater reserves alone. For instance, according to the Delhi Jal Board estimates, groundwater meets just 10% of Delhi&rsquo;s drinking water needs. The rest is met by surface water sources, most of it transported from outside Delhi. The urban needs, which underpin much reporting on &lsquo;water crises&rsquo;, need to be met by robust long-term planning and preparation for droughts and other contingencies.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Finally, we need to reconsider our approaches to water governance. We must recognise that the fulcrum of change and action is with the States. For long, water resource departments in States have continued to follow the conventional approaches of supply augmentation. The challenge is that of reorienting themselves towards deploying strategies of demand management, conservation and regulation. The Centre has to work with States towards an institutional change for the necessary course-shift. The Finance Minister, in her budget, repeatedly stated that the government will work with States to address India&rsquo;s national water security challenges. Let us hope that the government intends to ____________________ (A) federal governance of water resources towards long-term water security.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;Q1. What could be the appropriate title of this passage?</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"isComprehension\": false,\n          \"paragraph\": null,\n          \"paragraphText\": null,\n          \"fillUpsAnswers\": [],\n          \"nameText\": \"Directions (1-5): Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.\\nFor almost two decades, the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) has been reporting on the increasing number of over-exploited blocks across India, the ‘dark’ category blocks. The recent annual book of CGWB has reported 1,034 units, out of the 6,584 units it monitors, as over-exploited. If this is the ‘crisis’, then we have had it for long. What has this not received enough attention? Is it because these zones are not in cities?\\n\\nJust to be sure how critical the ‘crisis’ is, CGWB’s 2013 estimates say that the groundwater development in India is just about 62% of the utilisable groundwater reserves. Similarly, a recent report by the Central Water Commission, prepared in collaboration with the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), asserted that India is not yet in “water scarcity condition”. But it is certainly in a “water-stressed condition”, with reducing per capita water availability. Here, we certainly don’t mean to say that India can continue with the present ways of water management. We also cannot remain in a state of denial that a crisis is not in the making. However, certain steps need to be taken to ensure a more useful and productive discourse about water governance challenges.\\n\\nFirst, India needs to reconsider the institutional processes for dissemination of knowledge about water resource management. There is a certain amount of danger inherent in the casual manner in which knowledge about water resources is legitimised and consumed, particularly in these days of ‘viral’ information.\\n\\nSecond, we need to recognise the crisis is not as much of scarcity as of delivery. The challenge is to ensure an adequate access to quality water, more so in urban areas where inequities over space and time are acute. We need to also realise that with the country’s rapid urbanisation, demand cannot be met by groundwater reserves alone. For instance, according to the Delhi Jal Board estimates, groundwater meets just 10% of Delhi’s drinking water needs. The rest is met by surface water sources, most of it transported from outside Delhi. The urban needs, which underpin much reporting on ‘water crises’, need to be met by robust long-term planning and preparation for droughts and other contingencies.\\n\\nFinally, we need to reconsider our approaches to water governance. We must recognise that the fulcrum of change and action is with the States. For long, water resource departments in States have continued to follow the conventional approaches of supply augmentation. The challenge is that of reorienting themselves towards deploying strategies of demand management, conservation and regulation. The Centre has to work with States towards an institutional change for the necessary course-shift. The Finance Minister, in her budget, repeatedly stated that the government will work with States to address India’s national water security challenges. Let us hope that the government intends to ____________________ (A) federal governance of water resources towards long-term water security.\\n \\n \\n Q1. What could be the appropriate title of this passage?\",\n          \"sectionId\": \"60f69fb8e70c56aee9070d22\",\n          \"order\": 1,\n          \"questionSubmitOrder\": 1,\n          \"options\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Water crisis</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Water crisis\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa831c651e1f\",\n              \"isMarked\": true\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">water crisis: Inadequate rainfall</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"water crisis: Inadequate rainfall\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa3a9d651e20\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Preserving water</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Preserving water\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa8287651e21\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Water governance strategies</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Water governance strategies\",\n              \"isCorrect\": true,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aaf9b6651e22\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">None of These</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"None of These\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa9ce3651e23\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"solution\": \"<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">Refer to the last and second last paragraph of this passage, it states that crisis of water is not due to scarcity but it is due to lack of preservation measures for delivering the same. As only groundwater is not sufficient due to expanding urbanisation, there needs to be long term planning and alongside staying prepared for drought. Also, in the last paragraph, the author says for reconsidering the strategies for water governance. For coping up with water security challenges, the Centre needs to collaborate with States strengthening the water resources governance and ensuring water security in the long term. Rest of the options are not specific enough to portray the passage as a whole. Thus, option (d) is the most suitable title for the above passage</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"hasMultipleAnswers\": false,\n          \"type\": \"multiple_choice\",\n          \"isAttempted\": true,\n          \"isPartiallyCorrect\": false,\n          \"isCorrect\": false,\n          \"isGraceMarked\": false,\n          \"isReevaluated\": false,\n          \"isBookmark\": false,\n          \"studentQuestionId\": \"6ab02797e59d371ca23ba719\",\n          \"marks\": {\n            \"positive\": 0,\n            \"negative\": 0.25\n          },\n          \"timeTaken\": 60000,\n          \"markForReview\": false,\n          \"isGpscType\": false,\n          \"isSurrendered\": false,\n          \"difficultyLevel\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"_id\": \"60f69fd0104d4374d476b0b1\",\n          \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Directions (1-5):&nbsp;Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">For almost two decades, the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) has been reporting on the increasing number of over-exploited blocks across India, the &lsquo;dark&rsquo; category blocks. The recent annual book of CGWB has reported 1,034 units, out of the 6,584 units it monitors, as over-exploited. If this is the &lsquo;crisis&rsquo;, then we have had it for long. What has this not received enough attention? Is it because these zones are not in cities?</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Just to be sure how critical the &lsquo;crisis&rsquo; is, CGWB&rsquo;s 2013 estimates say that the groundwater development in India is just about 62% of the utilisable groundwater reserves. Similarly, a recent report by the Central Water Commission, prepared in collaboration with the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), asserted that India is not yet in &ldquo;water scarcity condition&rdquo;. But it is certainly in a &ldquo;water-stressed condition&rdquo;, with reducing per capita water availability. Here, we certainly don&rsquo;t mean to say that India can continue with the present ways of water management. We also cannot remain in a state of denial that a crisis is not in the making. However, certain steps need to be taken to ensure a more useful and productive discourse about water governance challenges.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">First, India needs to reconsider the institutional processes for dissemination of knowledge about water resource management. There is a certain amount of danger inherent in the casual manner in which knowledge about water resources is legitimised and consumed, particularly in these days of &lsquo;viral&rsquo; information.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /></span></strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Second,&nbsp;<em>we need to recognise the crisis is not as much of scarcity as of delivery.</em>&nbsp;The challenge is to ensure an adequate access to quality water, more so in urban areas where inequities over space and time are acute. We need to also realise that with the country&rsquo;s rapid urbanisation, demand cannot be met by groundwater reserves alone. For instance, according to the Delhi Jal Board estimates, groundwater meets just 10% of Delhi&rsquo;s drinking water needs. The rest is met by surface water sources, most of it transported from outside Delhi. The urban needs, which underpin much reporting on &lsquo;water crises&rsquo;, need to be met by robust long-term planning and preparation for droughts and other contingencies.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Finally, we need to reconsider our approaches to water governance. We must recognise that the fulcrum of change and action is with the States. For long, water resource departments in States have continued to follow the conventional approaches of supply augmentation. The challenge is that of reorienting themselves towards deploying strategies of demand management, conservation and regulation. The Centre has to work with States towards an institutional change for the necessary course-shift. The Finance Minister, in her budget, repeatedly stated that the government will work with States to address India&rsquo;s national water security challenges. Let us hope that the government intends to ____________________ (A) federal governance of water resources towards long-term water security.</span></strong></p>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<p><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">2.What does the italicized sentence <em>&lsquo;we need to recognise the crisis is not as much of scarcity as of delivery&rsquo;</em> Refers to as per the above passage?</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"isComprehension\": false,\n          \"paragraph\": null,\n          \"paragraphText\": null,\n          \"fillUpsAnswers\": [],\n          \"nameText\": \"Directions (1-5): Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.\\nFor almost two decades, the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) has been reporting on the increasing number of over-exploited blocks across India, the ‘dark’ category blocks. The recent annual book of CGWB has reported 1,034 units, out of the 6,584 units it monitors, as over-exploited. If this is the ‘crisis’, then we have had it for long. What has this not received enough attention? Is it because these zones are not in cities?\\n\\nJust to be sure how critical the ‘crisis’ is, CGWB’s 2013 estimates say that the groundwater development in India is just about 62% of the utilisable groundwater reserves. Similarly, a recent report by the Central Water Commission, prepared in collaboration with the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), asserted that India is not yet in “water scarcity condition”. But it is certainly in a “water-stressed condition”, with reducing per capita water availability. Here, we certainly don’t mean to say that India can continue with the present ways of water management. We also cannot remain in a state of denial that a crisis is not in the making. However, certain steps need to be taken to ensure a more useful and productive discourse about water governance challenges.\\n\\nFirst, India needs to reconsider the institutional processes for dissemination of knowledge about water resource management. There is a certain amount of danger inherent in the casual manner in which knowledge about water resources is legitimised and consumed, particularly in these days of ‘viral’ information.\\n\\nSecond, we need to recognise the crisis is not as much of scarcity as of delivery. The challenge is to ensure an adequate access to quality water, more so in urban areas where inequities over space and time are acute. We need to also realise that with the country’s rapid urbanisation, demand cannot be met by groundwater reserves alone. For instance, according to the Delhi Jal Board estimates, groundwater meets just 10% of Delhi’s drinking water needs. The rest is met by surface water sources, most of it transported from outside Delhi. The urban needs, which underpin much reporting on ‘water crises’, need to be met by robust long-term planning and preparation for droughts and other contingencies.\\n\\nFinally, we need to reconsider our approaches to water governance. We must recognise that the fulcrum of change and action is with the States. For long, water resource departments in States have continued to follow the conventional approaches of supply augmentation. The challenge is that of reorienting themselves towards deploying strategies of demand management, conservation and regulation. The Centre has to work with States towards an institutional change for the necessary course-shift. The Finance Minister, in her budget, repeatedly stated that the government will work with States to address India’s national water security challenges. Let us hope that the government intends to ____________________ (A) federal governance of water resources towards long-term water security.\\n \\n2.What does the italicized sentence ‘we need to recognise the crisis is not as much of scarcity as of delivery’ Refers to as per the above passage?\",\n          \"sectionId\": \"60f69fb8e70c56aee9070d22\",\n          \"order\": 2,\n          \"questionSubmitOrder\": 2,\n          \"options\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;It is not about water scarcity but water availability. Water is not scarce but there is a lack of strategies to get it properly delivered</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \" It is not about water scarcity but water availability. Water is not scarce but there is a lack of strategies to get it properly delivered\",\n              \"isCorrect\": true,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa11e0651e01\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">With expanding urbanisation, there is a need to generate methods to access quality water as ground water is not sufficient to meet drinking water needs.</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"With expanding urbanisation, there is a need to generate methods to access quality water as ground water is not sufficient to meet drinking water needs.\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa6bed651e02\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Surface water is sufficient to meet the demands of drinking water</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Surface water is sufficient to meet the demands of drinking water\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa6eba651e03\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Without denying the fact that water crisis is in process, necessary steps must be taken ensuring productive discourse and effective dealing with water governance challenges.</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Without denying the fact that water crisis is in process, necessary steps must be taken ensuring productive discourse and effective dealing with water governance challenges.\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aaad89651e04\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">All of the above</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"All of the above\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa1c76651e05\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"solution\": \"<p><strong style=\\\"font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-ansi-language: EN-IN; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN; mso-bidi-language: AR-SA;\\\">Refer to the fourth paragraph of the passage, it states that the main challenge is to deliver quality water, especially in urban areas. Lack of time and space makes it a cumbersome task to bring quality drinking water everywhere in an organized manner. To achieve this goal, there is a dire need for effective strategies. Hence, option (a) is most adequate answer</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"hasMultipleAnswers\": false,\n          \"type\": \"multiple_choice\",\n          \"isAttempted\": false,\n          \"isPartiallyCorrect\": false,\n          \"isCorrect\": false,\n          \"isGraceMarked\": false,\n          \"isReevaluated\": false,\n          \"isBookmark\": false,\n          \"studentQuestionId\": \"6ab02797e59d3708d83ba71a\",\n          \"marks\": {\n            \"positive\": 0,\n            \"negative\": 0\n          },\n          \"timeTaken\": 128000,\n          \"markForReview\": false,\n          \"isGpscType\": false,\n          \"isSurrendered\": false,\n          \"difficultyLevel\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"_id\": \"60f69fd0104d43502b76b0b6\",\n          \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Directions (1-5):&nbsp;Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">For almost two decades, the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) has been reporting on the increasing number of over-exploited blocks across India, the &lsquo;dark&rsquo; category blocks. The recent annual book of CGWB has reported 1,034 units, out of the 6,584 units it monitors, as over-exploited. If this is the &lsquo;crisis&rsquo;, then we have had it for long. What has this not received enough attention? Is it because these zones are not in cities?</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Just to be sure how critical the &lsquo;crisis&rsquo; is, CGWB&rsquo;s 2013 estimates say that the groundwater development in India is just about 62% of the utilisable groundwater reserves. Similarly, a recent report by the Central Water Commission, prepared in collaboration with the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), asserted that India is not yet in &ldquo;water scarcity condition&rdquo;. But it is certainly in a &ldquo;water-stressed condition&rdquo;, with reducing per capita water availability. Here, we certainly don&rsquo;t mean to say that India can continue with the present ways of water management. We also cannot remain in a state of denial that a crisis is not in the making. However, certain steps need to be taken to ensure a more useful and productive discourse about water governance challenges.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">First, India needs to reconsider the institutional processes for dissemination of knowledge about water resource management. There is a certain amount of danger inherent in the casual manner in which knowledge about water resources is legitimised and consumed, particularly in these days of &lsquo;viral&rsquo; information.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /></span></strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Second,&nbsp;<em>we need to recognise the crisis is not as much of scarcity as of delivery.</em>&nbsp;The challenge is to ensure an adequate access to quality water, more so in urban areas where inequities over space and time are acute. We need to also realise that with the country&rsquo;s rapid urbanisation, demand cannot be met by groundwater reserves alone. For instance, according to the Delhi Jal Board estimates, groundwater meets just 10% of Delhi&rsquo;s drinking water needs. The rest is met by surface water sources, most of it transported from outside Delhi. The urban needs, which underpin much reporting on &lsquo;water crises&rsquo;, need to be met by robust long-term planning and preparation for droughts and other contingencies.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Finally, we need to reconsider our approaches to water governance. We must recognise that the fulcrum of change and action is with the States. For long, water resource departments in States have continued to follow the conventional approaches of supply augmentation. The challenge is that of reorienting themselves towards deploying strategies of demand management, conservation and regulation. The Centre has to work with States towards an institutional change for the necessary course-shift. The Finance Minister, in her budget, repeatedly stated that the government will work with States to address India&rsquo;s national water security challenges. Let us hope that the government intends to ____________________ (A) federal governance of water resources towards long-term water security.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\">&nbsp;</p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Q3. As per the passage, what are the steps taken to ensure maximum fulfilment of water governance?</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"isComprehension\": false,\n          \"paragraph\": null,\n          \"paragraphText\": null,\n          \"fillUpsAnswers\": [],\n          \"nameText\": \"Directions (1-5): Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.\\nFor almost two decades, the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) has been reporting on the increasing number of over-exploited blocks across India, the ‘dark’ category blocks. The recent annual book of CGWB has reported 1,034 units, out of the 6,584 units it monitors, as over-exploited. If this is the ‘crisis’, then we have had it for long. What has this not received enough attention? Is it because these zones are not in cities?\\n\\nJust to be sure how critical the ‘crisis’ is, CGWB’s 2013 estimates say that the groundwater development in India is just about 62% of the utilisable groundwater reserves. Similarly, a recent report by the Central Water Commission, prepared in collaboration with the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), asserted that India is not yet in “water scarcity condition”. But it is certainly in a “water-stressed condition”, with reducing per capita water availability. Here, we certainly don’t mean to say that India can continue with the present ways of water management. We also cannot remain in a state of denial that a crisis is not in the making. However, certain steps need to be taken to ensure a more useful and productive discourse about water governance challenges.\\n\\nFirst, India needs to reconsider the institutional processes for dissemination of knowledge about water resource management. There is a certain amount of danger inherent in the casual manner in which knowledge about water resources is legitimised and consumed, particularly in these days of ‘viral’ information.\\n\\nSecond, we need to recognise the crisis is not as much of scarcity as of delivery. The challenge is to ensure an adequate access to quality water, more so in urban areas where inequities over space and time are acute. We need to also realise that with the country’s rapid urbanisation, demand cannot be met by groundwater reserves alone. For instance, according to the Delhi Jal Board estimates, groundwater meets just 10% of Delhi’s drinking water needs. The rest is met by surface water sources, most of it transported from outside Delhi. The urban needs, which underpin much reporting on ‘water crises’, need to be met by robust long-term planning and preparation for droughts and other contingencies.\\n\\nFinally, we need to reconsider our approaches to water governance. We must recognise that the fulcrum of change and action is with the States. For long, water resource departments in States have continued to follow the conventional approaches of supply augmentation. The challenge is that of reorienting themselves towards deploying strategies of demand management, conservation and regulation. The Centre has to work with States towards an institutional change for the necessary course-shift. The Finance Minister, in her budget, repeatedly stated that the government will work with States to address India’s national water security challenges. Let us hope that the government intends to ____________________ (A) federal governance of water resources towards long-term water security.\\n \\nQ3. As per the passage, what are the steps taken to ensure maximum fulfilment of water governance?\",\n          \"sectionId\": \"60f69fb8e70c56aee9070d22\",\n          \"order\": 3,\n          \"questionSubmitOrder\": 3,\n          \"options\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Spreading the knowledge about water resource management</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Spreading the knowledge about water resource management\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa15f5651de3\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Providing adequate access to quality water</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Providing adequate access to quality water\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa19c4651de4\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Changing the conventional approaches of supply augmentation</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Changing the conventional approaches of supply augmentation\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa4773651de5\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">All of the above</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"All of the above\",\n              \"isCorrect\": true,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa8656651de6\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">None of these</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"None of these\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa3ac2651de7\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"solution\": \"<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">Refer to the, third, fourth and fifth paragraph of the above passage, the steps necessary to reach maximum results fulfilling challenges of water governance are given. Hence, option (d) is the right answer choice</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"hasMultipleAnswers\": false,\n          \"type\": \"multiple_choice\",\n          \"isAttempted\": false,\n          \"isPartiallyCorrect\": false,\n          \"isCorrect\": false,\n          \"isGraceMarked\": false,\n          \"isReevaluated\": false,\n          \"isBookmark\": false,\n          \"studentQuestionId\": \"6ab02797e59d3738d53ba71b\",\n          \"marks\": {\n            \"positive\": 0,\n            \"negative\": 0\n          },\n          \"timeTaken\": 0,\n          \"markForReview\": false,\n          \"isGpscType\": false,\n          \"isSurrendered\": false,\n          \"difficultyLevel\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"_id\": \"60f69fd0104d43111676b0bb\",\n          \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Directions (1-5):&nbsp;Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">For almost two decades, the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) has been reporting on the increasing number of over-exploited blocks across India, the &lsquo;dark&rsquo; category blocks. The recent annual book of CGWB has reported 1,034 units, out of the 6,584 units it monitors, as over-exploited. If this is the &lsquo;crisis&rsquo;, then we have had it for long. What has this not received enough attention? Is it because these zones are not in cities?</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Just to be sure how critical the &lsquo;crisis&rsquo; is, CGWB&rsquo;s 2013 estimates say that the groundwater development in India is just about 62% of the utilisable groundwater reserves. Similarly, a recent report by the Central Water Commission, prepared in collaboration with the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), asserted that India is not yet in &ldquo;water scarcity condition&rdquo;. But it is certainly in a &ldquo;water-stressed condition&rdquo;, with reducing per capita water availability. Here, we certainly don&rsquo;t mean to say that India can continue with the present ways of water management. We also cannot remain in a state of denial that a crisis is not in the making. However, certain steps need to be taken to ensure a more useful and productive discourse about water governance challenges.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">First, India needs to reconsider the institutional processes for dissemination of knowledge about water resource management. There is a certain amount of danger inherent in the casual manner in which knowledge about water resources is legitimised and consumed, particularly in these days of &lsquo;viral&rsquo; information.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /></span></strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Second,&nbsp;<em>we need to recognise the crisis is not as much of scarcity as of delivery.</em>&nbsp;The challenge is to ensure an adequate access to quality water, more so in urban areas where inequities over space and time are acute. We need to also realise that with the country&rsquo;s rapid urbanisation, demand cannot be met by groundwater reserves alone. For instance, according to the Delhi Jal Board estimates, groundwater meets just 10% of Delhi&rsquo;s drinking water needs. The rest is met by surface water sources, most of it transported from outside Delhi. The urban needs, which underpin much reporting on &lsquo;water crises&rsquo;, need to be met by robust long-term planning and preparation for droughts and other contingencies.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Finally, we need to reconsider our approaches to water governance. We must recognise that the fulcrum of change and action is with the States. For long, water resource departments in States have continued to follow the conventional approaches of supply augmentation. The challenge is that of reorienting themselves towards deploying strategies of demand management, conservation and regulation. The Centre has to work with States towards an institutional change for the necessary course-shift. The Finance Minister, in her budget, repeatedly stated that the government will work with States to address India&rsquo;s national water security challenges. Let us hope that the government intends to ____________________ (A) federal governance of water resources towards long-term water security.</span></strong></p>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Q4. Which of the following statements are not true according to the above passage?</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"isComprehension\": false,\n          \"paragraph\": null,\n          \"paragraphText\": null,\n          \"fillUpsAnswers\": [],\n          \"nameText\": \"Directions (1-5): Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.\\nFor almost two decades, the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) has been reporting on the increasing number of over-exploited blocks across India, the ‘dark’ category blocks. The recent annual book of CGWB has reported 1,034 units, out of the 6,584 units it monitors, as over-exploited. If this is the ‘crisis’, then we have had it for long. What has this not received enough attention? Is it because these zones are not in cities?\\n\\nJust to be sure how critical the ‘crisis’ is, CGWB’s 2013 estimates say that the groundwater development in India is just about 62% of the utilisable groundwater reserves. Similarly, a recent report by the Central Water Commission, prepared in collaboration with the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), asserted that India is not yet in “water scarcity condition”. But it is certainly in a “water-stressed condition”, with reducing per capita water availability. Here, we certainly don’t mean to say that India can continue with the present ways of water management. We also cannot remain in a state of denial that a crisis is not in the making. However, certain steps need to be taken to ensure a more useful and productive discourse about water governance challenges.\\n\\nFirst, India needs to reconsider the institutional processes for dissemination of knowledge about water resource management. There is a certain amount of danger inherent in the casual manner in which knowledge about water resources is legitimised and consumed, particularly in these days of ‘viral’ information.\\n\\nSecond, we need to recognise the crisis is not as much of scarcity as of delivery. The challenge is to ensure an adequate access to quality water, more so in urban areas where inequities over space and time are acute. We need to also realise that with the country’s rapid urbanisation, demand cannot be met by groundwater reserves alone. For instance, according to the Delhi Jal Board estimates, groundwater meets just 10% of Delhi’s drinking water needs. The rest is met by surface water sources, most of it transported from outside Delhi. The urban needs, which underpin much reporting on ‘water crises’, need to be met by robust long-term planning and preparation for droughts and other contingencies.\\n\\nFinally, we need to reconsider our approaches to water governance. We must recognise that the fulcrum of change and action is with the States. For long, water resource departments in States have continued to follow the conventional approaches of supply augmentation. The challenge is that of reorienting themselves towards deploying strategies of demand management, conservation and regulation. The Centre has to work with States towards an institutional change for the necessary course-shift. The Finance Minister, in her budget, repeatedly stated that the government will work with States to address India’s national water security challenges. Let us hope that the government intends to ____________________ (A) federal governance of water resources towards long-term water security.\\n \\nQ4. Which of the following statements are not true according to the above passage?\",\n          \"sectionId\": \"60f69fb8e70c56aee9070d22\",\n          \"order\": 4,\n          \"questionSubmitOrder\": 4,\n          \"options\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Conventional methods of supply escalation have been acquired by water resource departments in States.</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Conventional methods of supply escalation have been acquired by water resource departments in States.\",\n              \"isCorrect\": true,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa2517651e10\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The knowledge about legitimization and consumption of water resources is circulated in a casual manner</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"The knowledge about legitimization and consumption of water resources is circulated in a casual manner\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa37f9651e11\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Only 10% of Delhi&rsquo;s drinking water needs are fulfilled by ground water</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Only 10% of Delhi’s drinking water needs are fulfilled by ground water\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa7072651e12\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;In urban areas, providing access to sufficient quality water is a challenge due to acute time and space.</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \" In urban areas, providing access to sufficient quality water is a challenge due to acute time and space.\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa5a8a651e13\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">None of these</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"None of these\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa4746651e14\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"solution\": \"<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">With the given information about water governance strategies, all the options are correct. Therefore, none of them is false. Option (e) is the apt choice.</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"hasMultipleAnswers\": false,\n          \"type\": \"multiple_choice\",\n          \"isAttempted\": false,\n          \"isPartiallyCorrect\": false,\n          \"isCorrect\": false,\n          \"isGraceMarked\": false,\n          \"isReevaluated\": false,\n          \"isBookmark\": false,\n          \"studentQuestionId\": \"6ab02797e59d3774743ba71c\",\n          \"marks\": {\n            \"positive\": 0,\n            \"negative\": 0\n          },\n          \"timeTaken\": 0,\n          \"markForReview\": false,\n          \"isGpscType\": false,\n          \"isSurrendered\": false,\n          \"difficultyLevel\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"_id\": \"60f69fd0104d4358ff76b0c0\",\n          \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Directions (1-5):&nbsp;Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">For almost two decades, the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) has been reporting on the increasing number of over-exploited blocks across India, the &lsquo;dark&rsquo; category blocks. The recent annual book of CGWB has reported 1,034 units, out of the 6,584 units it monitors, as over-exploited. If this is the &lsquo;crisis&rsquo;, then we have had it for long. What has this not received enough attention? Is it because these zones are not in cities?</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Just to be sure how critical the &lsquo;crisis&rsquo; is, CGWB&rsquo;s 2013 estimates say that the groundwater development in India is just about 62% of the utilisable groundwater reserves. Similarly, a recent report by the Central Water Commission, prepared in collaboration with the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), asserted that India is not yet in &ldquo;water scarcity condition&rdquo;. But it is certainly in a &ldquo;water-stressed condition&rdquo;, with reducing per capita water availability. Here, we certainly don&rsquo;t mean to say that India can continue with the present ways of water management. We also cannot remain in a state of denial that a crisis is not in the making. However, certain steps need to be taken to ensure a more useful and productive discourse about water governance challenges.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">First, India needs to reconsider the institutional processes for dissemination of knowledge about water resource management. There is a certain amount of danger inherent in the casual manner in which knowledge about water resources is legitimised and consumed, particularly in these days of &lsquo;viral&rsquo; information.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /></span></strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Second,&nbsp;<em>we need to recognise the crisis is not as much of scarcity as of delivery.</em>&nbsp;The challenge is to ensure an adequate access to quality water, more so in urban areas where inequities over space and time are acute. We need to also realise that with the country&rsquo;s rapid urbanisation, demand cannot be met by groundwater reserves alone. For instance, according to the Delhi Jal Board estimates, groundwater meets just 10% of Delhi&rsquo;s drinking water needs. The rest is met by surface water sources, most of it transported from outside Delhi. The urban needs, which underpin much reporting on &lsquo;water crises&rsquo;, need to be met by robust long-term planning and preparation for droughts and other contingencies.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Finally, we need to reconsider our approaches to water governance. We must recognise that the fulcrum of change and action is with the States. For long, water resource departments in States have continued to follow the conventional approaches of supply augmentation. The challenge is that of reorienting themselves towards deploying strategies of demand management, conservation and regulation. The Centre has to work with States towards an institutional change for the necessary course-shift. The Finance Minister, in her budget, repeatedly stated that the government will work with States to address India&rsquo;s national water security challenges. Let us hope that the government intends to ____________________ (A) federal governance of water resources towards long-term water security.</span></strong></p>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<p><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Q5. Which of the following is the most suitable word to fill the blank (A) as given in the passage.</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"isComprehension\": false,\n          \"paragraph\": null,\n          \"paragraphText\": null,\n          \"fillUpsAnswers\": [],\n          \"nameText\": \"Directions (1-5): Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.\\nFor almost two decades, the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) has been reporting on the increasing number of over-exploited blocks across India, the ‘dark’ category blocks. The recent annual book of CGWB has reported 1,034 units, out of the 6,584 units it monitors, as over-exploited. If this is the ‘crisis’, then we have had it for long. What has this not received enough attention? Is it because these zones are not in cities?\\n\\nJust to be sure how critical the ‘crisis’ is, CGWB’s 2013 estimates say that the groundwater development in India is just about 62% of the utilisable groundwater reserves. Similarly, a recent report by the Central Water Commission, prepared in collaboration with the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), asserted that India is not yet in “water scarcity condition”. But it is certainly in a “water-stressed condition”, with reducing per capita water availability. Here, we certainly don’t mean to say that India can continue with the present ways of water management. We also cannot remain in a state of denial that a crisis is not in the making. However, certain steps need to be taken to ensure a more useful and productive discourse about water governance challenges.\\n\\nFirst, India needs to reconsider the institutional processes for dissemination of knowledge about water resource management. There is a certain amount of danger inherent in the casual manner in which knowledge about water resources is legitimised and consumed, particularly in these days of ‘viral’ information.\\n\\nSecond, we need to recognise the crisis is not as much of scarcity as of delivery. The challenge is to ensure an adequate access to quality water, more so in urban areas where inequities over space and time are acute. We need to also realise that with the country’s rapid urbanisation, demand cannot be met by groundwater reserves alone. For instance, according to the Delhi Jal Board estimates, groundwater meets just 10% of Delhi’s drinking water needs. The rest is met by surface water sources, most of it transported from outside Delhi. The urban needs, which underpin much reporting on ‘water crises’, need to be met by robust long-term planning and preparation for droughts and other contingencies.\\n\\nFinally, we need to reconsider our approaches to water governance. We must recognise that the fulcrum of change and action is with the States. For long, water resource departments in States have continued to follow the conventional approaches of supply augmentation. The challenge is that of reorienting themselves towards deploying strategies of demand management, conservation and regulation. The Centre has to work with States towards an institutional change for the necessary course-shift. The Finance Minister, in her budget, repeatedly stated that the government will work with States to address India’s national water security challenges. Let us hope that the government intends to ____________________ (A) federal governance of water resources towards long-term water security.\\n \\nQ5. Which of the following is the most suitable word to fill the blank (A) as given in the passage.\",\n          \"sectionId\": \"60f69fb8e70c56aee9070d22\",\n          \"order\": 5,\n          \"questionSubmitOrder\": 5,\n          \"options\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Approach</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Approach\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa2be6651df2\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Destruct</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Destruct\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa2267651df3\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Strengthen</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Strengthen\",\n              \"isCorrect\": true,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aaff0e651df4\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Uncertain</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Uncertain\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa3b69651df5\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Tinker</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Tinker\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa5b11651df6\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"solution\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">Option (c) , &lsquo;Strengthen&rsquo; is the most suitable word to fit in the blank (A). It makes the sentence contextually and grammatically correct.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">(a)Approach-Come near or nearer to</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">(b)Destruct-Cause deliberate, terminal damage to</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;(c)Uncertain-Not able to be relied on</span></strong></p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">(d)Tinker-An act of attempting to repair something</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"hasMultipleAnswers\": false,\n          \"type\": \"multiple_choice\",\n          \"isAttempted\": false,\n          \"isPartiallyCorrect\": false,\n          \"isCorrect\": false,\n          \"isGraceMarked\": false,\n          \"isReevaluated\": false,\n          \"isBookmark\": false,\n          \"studentQuestionId\": \"6ab02797e59d37e7af3ba71d\",\n          \"marks\": {\n            \"positive\": 0,\n            \"negative\": 0\n          },\n          \"timeTaken\": 0,\n          \"markForReview\": false,\n          \"isGpscType\": false,\n          \"isSurrendered\": false,\n          \"difficultyLevel\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"_id\": \"60f69fd0104d436c3b76b0c5\",\n          \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Directions (6-10):&nbsp;Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">There has been much development recently with respect to the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012. With an objective of stopping the rampant sexual abuse of children, the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (Amendment) Bill, 2019 was introduced in the Rajya Sabha in July, and later passed by both Houses of Parliament. It is all set to become the &lsquo;law of the land&rsquo;. The present bill is welcome in certain respects as it specifically defines what &lsquo;child pornography&rsquo; is; &lsquo;using a child for pornographic purposes&rsquo; and for &lsquo;possessing or storing pornography involving a child&rsquo; is punishable. It has also widened the ambit of &lsquo;Aggravated sexual assault&rsquo;.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The highlight of the Bill is the introduction of the death penalty for the rape of minors. The Bill, in its object clause, justifies this by referring to the judgments of the Supreme Court in Machhi Singh (1983) and Devender Pal Singh (2002) in which the court has held that the death penalty can be awarded only in rarest of rare cases. Thus the intention of the Bill is to have a deterrent effect; but it can be argued that the introduction of the death penalty may backfire in cases of child sexual abuse and even have a catastrophic effect. Often, the perpetrators of abuse are family members and having such penalty in the statute book may discourage the registration of the crime itself. Also, it may threaten the life of the minor as the maximum punishment for murder is also the death sentence.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The Justice J.S. Verma Committee, which was constituted in 2013 in the aftermath of the Nirbhaya case, after due deliberations found itself against the imposition of death penalty in rape cases. The 262nd Report of the Law Commission of India, 2015, also provides for abolition of the death penalty except in terror cases.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><em><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Today, the death penalty has become a prominent tool of symbolic legislation</span></em></strong><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;&mdash; a political statement indeed. Many a time, the Government, by introducing the death penalty, portrays itself to be strict and serious with regard to such offences. It largely diverts attention from the core issues of infrastructural apathy, procedural lapses and trial delays and conveniently evades the fact that &lsquo;it is the certainty of punishment rather than its severity which has deterrence in real sense&rsquo;. It is pertinent to note here that even a year-and-a-half after the passage of the Criminal Law (Amendment) Bill, 2018, which introduced the death penalty for rape of a minor girl, such incidents have not been under check. The debate here is not about retaining or abolishing the death penalty but the probable _________________________(A) of its provision in the Act.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The Supreme Court has recently taken cognisance of the sexual abuse of children, directing its registry to file a case as writ petition with cause title &ldquo;In-re Alarming Rise in The Number of Reported Child Rape Incidents&rdquo;. The court has also observed that it intends having a &lsquo;zero tolerance policy&rsquo; toward child rape. As data on sexual crimes against children collected by the court show, 24,212 FIRs were filed across India from January to June this year. According to National Crime Records Bureau data of 2016, the conviction rate in POCSO cases is 29.6% while pendency is as high as 89%. The prescribed time period of two months for trial in such cases is hardly complied with.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The court has also taken note of the delay in trials, in turn directing the Central Government to set up special courts within 60 days of the order in each district having more than 100 pending cases under the Act. It is to be seen how long it takes to comply with the order.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2018 introduced the death penalty for rape of girls below the age of 12. At the same time, the POCSO Act, under Section 42, provides that where the same act constitutes an offence under the said Act and any other law, then the offender will be punished under the Act or such law, whichever provides for greater punishment. This has created an issue as the effect of such an amendment was death penalty for rape of minor girls but not for assault against minor boys.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /></span></strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><strong>The proposed Bill does away with such a discrepancy. It is gender neutral and provides for the death penalty for &ldquo;aggravated penetrative sexual assault of a child&rdquo;, thus bringing both these pieces of legislation on a par with each other in this respect. With these amendments and with the Supreme Court considering child abuse &ldquo;intolerable&rdquo;, there seems to be reasonable hope now that vulnerable children could be safer. The Bill is a step forward in preventing child abuse but the consequences of providing for the death penalty need to be closely observed.</strong></span></p>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;Q6. What could be the most appropriate title of this passage?</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"isComprehension\": false,\n          \"paragraph\": null,\n          \"paragraphText\": null,\n          \"fillUpsAnswers\": [],\n          \"nameText\": \"Directions (6-10): Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.\\nThere has been much development recently with respect to the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012. With an objective of stopping the rampant sexual abuse of children, the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (Amendment) Bill, 2019 was introduced in the Rajya Sabha in July, and later passed by both Houses of Parliament. It is all set to become the ‘law of the land’. The present bill is welcome in certain respects as it specifically defines what ‘child pornography’ is; ‘using a child for pornographic purposes’ and for ‘possessing or storing pornography involving a child’ is punishable. It has also widened the ambit of ‘Aggravated sexual assault’.\\n\\nThe highlight of the Bill is the introduction of the death penalty for the rape of minors. The Bill, in its object clause, justifies this by referring to the judgments of the Supreme Court in Machhi Singh (1983) and Devender Pal Singh (2002) in which the court has held that the death penalty can be awarded only in rarest of rare cases. Thus the intention of the Bill is to have a deterrent effect; but it can be argued that the introduction of the death penalty may backfire in cases of child sexual abuse and even have a catastrophic effect. Often, the perpetrators of abuse are family members and having such penalty in the statute book may discourage the registration of the crime itself. Also, it may threaten the life of the minor as the maximum punishment for murder is also the death sentence.\\n \\nThe Justice J.S. Verma Committee, which was constituted in 2013 in the aftermath of the Nirbhaya case, after due deliberations found itself against the imposition of death penalty in rape cases. The 262nd Report of the Law Commission of India, 2015, also provides for abolition of the death penalty except in terror cases.\\nToday, the death penalty has become a prominent tool of symbolic legislation — a political statement indeed. Many a time, the Government, by introducing the death penalty, portrays itself to be strict and serious with regard to such offences. It largely diverts attention from the core issues of infrastructural apathy, procedural lapses and trial delays and conveniently evades the fact that ‘it is the certainty of punishment rather than its severity which has deterrence in real sense’. It is pertinent to note here that even a year-and-a-half after the passage of the Criminal Law (Amendment) Bill, 2018, which introduced the death penalty for rape of a minor girl, such incidents have not been under check. The debate here is not about retaining or abolishing the death penalty but the probable _________________________(A) of its provision in the Act.\\n\\nThe Supreme Court has recently taken cognisance of the sexual abuse of children, directing its registry to file a case as writ petition with cause title “In-re Alarming Rise in The Number of Reported Child Rape Incidents”. The court has also observed that it intends having a ‘zero tolerance policy’ toward child rape. As data on sexual crimes against children collected by the court show, 24,212 FIRs were filed across India from January to June this year. According to National Crime Records Bureau data of 2016, the conviction rate in POCSO cases is 29.6% while pendency is as high as 89%. The prescribed time period of two months for trial in such cases is hardly complied with.\\n \\nThe court has also taken note of the delay in trials, in turn directing the Central Government to set up special courts within 60 days of the order in each district having more than 100 pending cases under the Act. It is to be seen how long it takes to comply with the order.\\nThe Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2018 introduced the death penalty for rape of girls below the age of 12. At the same time, the POCSO Act, under Section 42, provides that where the same act constitutes an offence under the said Act and any other law, then the offender will be punished under the Act or such law, whichever provides for greater punishment. This has created an issue as the effect of such an amendment was death penalty for rape of minor girls but not for assault against minor boys.\\n \\nThe proposed Bill does away with such a discrepancy. It is gender neutral and provides for the death penalty for “aggravated penetrative sexual assault of a child”, thus bringing both these pieces of legislation on a par with each other in this respect. With these amendments and with the Supreme Court considering child abuse “intolerable”, there seems to be reasonable hope now that vulnerable children could be safer. The Bill is a step forward in preventing child abuse but the consequences of providing for the death penalty need to be closely observed.\\n \\n Q6. What could be the most appropriate title of this passage?\",\n          \"sectionId\": \"60f69fb8e70c56aee9070d22\",\n          \"order\": 6,\n          \"questionSubmitOrder\": 6,\n          \"options\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Pondering over POSCO Bill</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Pondering over POSCO Bill\",\n              \"isCorrect\": true,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aaacfa651e24\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Life sentence under POSCO Bill</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Life sentence under POSCO Bill\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa4fd3651e25\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Diverting attention from core issues under POSCO Bill</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Diverting attention from core issues under POSCO Bill\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa375d651e26\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">All of the above</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"All of the above\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa3c55651e27\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">None of These</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"None of These\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa17e5651e28\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"solution\": \"<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">Option (a) is the most suitable answer choice. It fits as the title of the passage because it states the entire theme behind this passage in few and precise words.</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"hasMultipleAnswers\": false,\n          \"type\": \"multiple_choice\",\n          \"isAttempted\": false,\n          \"isPartiallyCorrect\": false,\n          \"isCorrect\": false,\n          \"isGraceMarked\": false,\n          \"isReevaluated\": false,\n          \"isBookmark\": false,\n          \"studentQuestionId\": \"6ab02797e59d37fbbd3ba71e\",\n          \"marks\": {\n            \"positive\": 0,\n            \"negative\": 0\n          },\n          \"timeTaken\": 0,\n          \"markForReview\": false,\n          \"isGpscType\": false,\n          \"isSurrendered\": false,\n          \"difficultyLevel\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"_id\": \"60f69fd0104d433dbb76b0ca\",\n          \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Directions (6-10):&nbsp;Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">There has been much development recently with respect to the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012. With an objective of stopping the rampant sexual abuse of children, the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (Amendment) Bill, 2019 was introduced in the Rajya Sabha in July, and later passed by both Houses of Parliament. It is all set to become the &lsquo;law of the land&rsquo;. The present bill is welcome in certain respects as it specifically defines what &lsquo;child pornography&rsquo; is; &lsquo;using a child for pornographic purposes&rsquo; and for &lsquo;possessing or storing pornography involving a child&rsquo; is punishable. It has also widened the ambit of &lsquo;Aggravated sexual assault&rsquo;.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The highlight of the Bill is the introduction of the death penalty for the rape of minors. The Bill, in its object clause, justifies this by referring to the judgments of the Supreme Court in Machhi Singh (1983) and Devender Pal Singh (2002) in which the court has held that the death penalty can be awarded only in rarest of rare cases. Thus the intention of the Bill is to have a deterrent effect; but it can be argued that the introduction of the death penalty may backfire in cases of child sexual abuse and even have a catastrophic effect. Often, the perpetrators of abuse are family members and having such penalty in the statute book may discourage the registration of the crime itself. Also, it may threaten the life of the minor as the maximum punishment for murder is also the death sentence.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The Justice J.S. Verma Committee, which was constituted in 2013 in the aftermath of the Nirbhaya case, after due deliberations found itself against the imposition of death penalty in rape cases. The 262nd Report of the Law Commission of India, 2015, also provides for abolition of the death penalty except in terror cases.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><em><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Today, the death penalty has become a prominent tool of symbolic legislation</span></em></strong><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;&mdash; a political statement indeed. Many a time, the Government, by introducing the death penalty, portrays itself to be strict and serious with regard to such offences. It largely diverts attention from the core issues of infrastructural apathy, procedural lapses and trial delays and conveniently evades the fact that &lsquo;it is the certainty of punishment rather than its severity which has deterrence in real sense&rsquo;. It is pertinent to note here that even a year-and-a-half after the passage of the Criminal Law (Amendment) Bill, 2018, which introduced the death penalty for rape of a minor girl, such incidents have not been under check. The debate here is not about retaining or abolishing the death penalty but the probable _________________________(A) of its provision in the Act.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The Supreme Court has recently taken cognisance of the sexual abuse of children, directing its registry to file a case as writ petition with cause title &ldquo;In-re Alarming Rise in The Number of Reported Child Rape Incidents&rdquo;. The court has also observed that it intends having a &lsquo;zero tolerance policy&rsquo; toward child rape. As data on sexual crimes against children collected by the court show, 24,212 FIRs were filed across India from January to June this year. According to National Crime Records Bureau data of 2016, the conviction rate in POCSO cases is 29.6% while pendency is as high as 89%. The prescribed time period of two months for trial in such cases is hardly complied with.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The court has also taken note of the delay in trials, in turn directing the Central Government to set up special courts within 60 days of the order in each district having more than 100 pending cases under the Act. It is to be seen how long it takes to comply with the order.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2018 introduced the death penalty for rape of girls below the age of 12. At the same time, the POCSO Act, under Section 42, provides that where the same act constitutes an offence under the said Act and any other law, then the offender will be punished under the Act or such law, whichever provides for greater punishment. This has created an issue as the effect of such an amendment was death penalty for rape of minor girls but not for assault against minor boys.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /></span></strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /></span></p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 12pt; background-color: #f9f9f9; text-align: justify;\\\">The proposed Bill does away with such a discrepancy. It is gender neutral and provides for the death penalty for &ldquo;aggravated penetrative sexual assault of a child&rdquo;, thus bringing both these pieces of legislation on a par with each other in this respect. With these amendments and with the Supreme Court considering child abuse &ldquo;intolerable&rdquo;, there seems to be reasonable hope now that vulnerable children could be safer. The Bill is a step forward in preventing child abuse but the consequences of providing for the death penalty need to be closely observed.</strong></p>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Q7.In what aspects, the present Protection of Children from Sexual Offence (amendment) Bill, 2019 is welcomed?</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"isComprehension\": false,\n          \"paragraph\": null,\n          \"paragraphText\": null,\n          \"fillUpsAnswers\": [],\n          \"nameText\": \"Directions (6-10): Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.\\nThere has been much development recently with respect to the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012. With an objective of stopping the rampant sexual abuse of children, the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (Amendment) Bill, 2019 was introduced in the Rajya Sabha in July, and later passed by both Houses of Parliament. It is all set to become the ‘law of the land’. The present bill is welcome in certain respects as it specifically defines what ‘child pornography’ is; ‘using a child for pornographic purposes’ and for ‘possessing or storing pornography involving a child’ is punishable. It has also widened the ambit of ‘Aggravated sexual assault’.\\n\\nThe highlight of the Bill is the introduction of the death penalty for the rape of minors. The Bill, in its object clause, justifies this by referring to the judgments of the Supreme Court in Machhi Singh (1983) and Devender Pal Singh (2002) in which the court has held that the death penalty can be awarded only in rarest of rare cases. Thus the intention of the Bill is to have a deterrent effect; but it can be argued that the introduction of the death penalty may backfire in cases of child sexual abuse and even have a catastrophic effect. Often, the perpetrators of abuse are family members and having such penalty in the statute book may discourage the registration of the crime itself. Also, it may threaten the life of the minor as the maximum punishment for murder is also the death sentence.\\n \\nThe Justice J.S. Verma Committee, which was constituted in 2013 in the aftermath of the Nirbhaya case, after due deliberations found itself against the imposition of death penalty in rape cases. The 262nd Report of the Law Commission of India, 2015, also provides for abolition of the death penalty except in terror cases.\\nToday, the death penalty has become a prominent tool of symbolic legislation — a political statement indeed. Many a time, the Government, by introducing the death penalty, portrays itself to be strict and serious with regard to such offences. It largely diverts attention from the core issues of infrastructural apathy, procedural lapses and trial delays and conveniently evades the fact that ‘it is the certainty of punishment rather than its severity which has deterrence in real sense’. It is pertinent to note here that even a year-and-a-half after the passage of the Criminal Law (Amendment) Bill, 2018, which introduced the death penalty for rape of a minor girl, such incidents have not been under check. The debate here is not about retaining or abolishing the death penalty but the probable _________________________(A) of its provision in the Act.\\n\\nThe Supreme Court has recently taken cognisance of the sexual abuse of children, directing its registry to file a case as writ petition with cause title “In-re Alarming Rise in The Number of Reported Child Rape Incidents”. The court has also observed that it intends having a ‘zero tolerance policy’ toward child rape. As data on sexual crimes against children collected by the court show, 24,212 FIRs were filed across India from January to June this year. According to National Crime Records Bureau data of 2016, the conviction rate in POCSO cases is 29.6% while pendency is as high as 89%. The prescribed time period of two months for trial in such cases is hardly complied with.\\n \\nThe court has also taken note of the delay in trials, in turn directing the Central Government to set up special courts within 60 days of the order in each district having more than 100 pending cases under the Act. It is to be seen how long it takes to comply with the order.\\nThe Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2018 introduced the death penalty for rape of girls below the age of 12. At the same time, the POCSO Act, under Section 42, provides that where the same act constitutes an offence under the said Act and any other law, then the offender will be punished under the Act or such law, whichever provides for greater punishment. This has created an issue as the effect of such an amendment was death penalty for rape of minor girls but not for assault against minor boys.\\n \\n\\nThe proposed Bill does away with such a discrepancy. It is gender neutral and provides for the death penalty for “aggravated penetrative sexual assault of a child”, thus bringing both these pieces of legislation on a par with each other in this respect. With these amendments and with the Supreme Court considering child abuse “intolerable”, there seems to be reasonable hope now that vulnerable children could be safer. The Bill is a step forward in preventing child abuse but the consequences of providing for the death penalty need to be closely observed.\\n \\nQ7.In what aspects, the present Protection of Children from Sexual Offence (amendment) Bill, 2019 is welcomed?\",\n          \"sectionId\": \"60f69fb8e70c56aee9070d22\",\n          \"order\": 7,\n          \"questionSubmitOrder\": 7,\n          \"options\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">It defines child pornography specifically</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"It defines child pornography specifically\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aada4f651e15\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">It has broadened the domain of aggravated sexual assault</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"It has broadened the domain of aggravated sexual assault\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa647c651e16\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Death penalty for rape of minors is included in this Bill.</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Death penalty for rape of minors is included in this Bill.\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa7a1f651e17\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">All of the above</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"All of the above\",\n              \"isCorrect\": true,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa8487651e18\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">None of these</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"None of these\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa3f6a651e19\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"solution\": \"<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">Refer to the introductory paragraph of this passage. The elements are mentioned due o which POSCO Bill is welcomed. Hence, option (d) is the most suitable answer choice.</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"hasMultipleAnswers\": false,\n          \"type\": \"multiple_choice\",\n          \"isAttempted\": false,\n          \"isPartiallyCorrect\": false,\n          \"isCorrect\": false,\n          \"isGraceMarked\": false,\n          \"isReevaluated\": false,\n          \"isBookmark\": false,\n          \"studentQuestionId\": \"6ab02797e59d3712e23ba71f\",\n          \"marks\": {\n            \"positive\": 0,\n            \"negative\": 0\n          },\n          \"timeTaken\": 0,\n          \"markForReview\": false,\n          \"isGpscType\": false,\n          \"isSurrendered\": false,\n          \"difficultyLevel\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"_id\": \"60f69fd0104d4348dd76b0cf\",\n          \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Directions (6-10):&nbsp;Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">There has been much development recently with respect to the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012. With an objective of stopping the rampant sexual abuse of children, the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (Amendment) Bill, 2019 was introduced in the Rajya Sabha in July, and later passed by both Houses of Parliament. It is all set to become the &lsquo;law of the land&rsquo;. The present bill is welcome in certain respects as it specifically defines what &lsquo;child pornography&rsquo; is; &lsquo;using a child for pornographic purposes&rsquo; and for &lsquo;possessing or storing pornography involving a child&rsquo; is punishable. It has also widened the ambit of &lsquo;Aggravated sexual assault&rsquo;.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The highlight of the Bill is the introduction of the death penalty for the rape of minors. The Bill, in its object clause, justifies this by referring to the judgments of the Supreme Court in Machhi Singh (1983) and Devender Pal Singh (2002) in which the court has held that the death penalty can be awarded only in rarest of rare cases. Thus the intention of the Bill is to have a deterrent effect; but it can be argued that the introduction of the death penalty may backfire in cases of child sexual abuse and even have a catastrophic effect. Often, the perpetrators of abuse are family members and having such penalty in the statute book may discourage the registration of the crime itself. Also, it may threaten the life of the minor as the maximum punishment for murder is also the death sentence.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nb