mal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;All of the above</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \" All of the above\",\n              \"isCorrect\": true,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa6eee651deb\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">None of these</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"None of these\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa51b6651dec\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"solution\": \"<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">Refer to the third paragraph of this passage, where it elaborates the statement that death penalty is now a prominent tool symbolizing legislation. It helps politicians to divert mind of public from core issues of legislature. Hence, option (d) is the most suitable answer choice.</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"hasMultipleAnswers\": false,\n          \"type\": \"multiple_choice\",\n          \"isAttempted\": false,\n          \"isPartiallyCorrect\": false,\n          \"isCorrect\": false,\n          \"isGraceMarked\": false,\n          \"isReevaluated\": false,\n          \"isBookmark\": false,\n          \"studentQuestionId\": \"6ab02797e59d3791653ba720\",\n          \"marks\": {\n            \"positive\": 0,\n            \"negative\": 0\n          },\n          \"timeTaken\": 0,\n          \"markForReview\": false,\n          \"isGpscType\": false,\n          \"isSurrendered\": false,\n          \"difficultyLevel\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"_id\": \"60f69fd0104d43e87876b0d4\",\n          \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Directions (6-10):&nbsp;Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">There has been much development recently with respect to the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012. With an objective of stopping the rampant sexual abuse of children, the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (Amendment) Bill, 2019 was introduced in the Rajya Sabha in July, and later passed by both Houses of Parliament. It is all set to become the &lsquo;law of the land&rsquo;. The present bill is welcome in certain respects as it specifically defines what &lsquo;child pornography&rsquo; is; &lsquo;using a child for pornographic purposes&rsquo; and for &lsquo;possessing or storing pornography involving a child&rsquo; is punishable. It has also widened the ambit of &lsquo;Aggravated sexual assault&rsquo;.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><br /></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The highlight of the Bill is the introduction of the death penalty for the rape of minors. The Bill, in its object clause, justifies this by referring to the judgments of the Supreme Court in Machhi Singh (1983) and Devender Pal Singh (2002) in which the court has held that the death penalty can be awarded only in rarest of rare cases. Thus the intention of the Bill is to have a deterrent effect; but it can be argued that the introduction of the death penalty may backfire in cases of child sexual abuse and even have a catastrophic effect. Often, the perpetrators of abuse are family members and having such penalty in the statute book may discourage the registration of the crime itself. Also, it may threaten the life of the minor as the maximum punishment for murder is also the death sentence.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The Justice J.S. Verma Committee, which was constituted in 2013 in the aftermath of the Nirbhaya case, after due deliberations found itself against the imposition of death penalty in rape cases. The 262nd Report of the Law Commission of India, 2015, also provides for abolition of the death penalty except in terror cases.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><em><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Today, the death penalty has become a prominent tool of symbolic legislation</span></em></strong><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;&mdash; a political statement indeed. Many a time, the Government, by introducing the death penalty, portrays itself to be strict and serious with regard to such offences. It largely diverts attention from the core issues of infrastructural apathy, procedural lapses and trial delays and conveniently evades the fact that &lsquo;it is the certainty of punishment rather than its severity which has deterrence in real sense&rsquo;. It is pertinent to note here that even a year-and-a-half after the passage of the Criminal Law (Amendment) Bill, 2018, which introduced the death penalty for rape of a minor girl, such incidents have not been under check. The debate here is not about retaining or abolishing the death penalty but the probable _________________________(A) of its provision in the Act.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><br /></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The Supreme Court has recently taken cognisance of the sexual abuse of children, directing its registry to file a case as writ petition with cause title &ldquo;In-re Alarming Rise in The Number of Reported Child Rape Incidents&rdquo;. The court has also observed that it intends having a &lsquo;zero tolerance policy&rsquo; toward child rape. As data on sexual crimes against children collected by the court show, 24,212 FIRs were filed across India from January to June this year. According to National Crime Records Bureau data of 2016, the conviction rate in POCSO cases is 29.6% while pendency is as high as 89%. The prescribed time period of two months for trial in such cases is hardly complied with.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The court has also taken note of the delay in trials, in turn directing the Central Government to set up special courts within 60 days of the order in each district having more than 100 pending cases under the Act. It is to be seen how long it takes to comply with the order.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2018 introduced the death penalty for rape of girls below the age of 12. At the same time, the POCSO Act, under Section 42, provides that where the same act constitutes an offence under the said Act and any other law, then the offender will be punished under the Act or such law, whichever provides for greater punishment. This has created an issue as the effect of such an amendment was death penalty for rape of minor girls but not for assault against minor boys.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /></span></strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /></span></p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 12pt; background-color: #f9f9f9; text-align: justify;\\\">The proposed Bill does away with such a discrepancy. It is gender neutral and provides for the death penalty for &ldquo;aggravated penetrative sexual assault of a child&rdquo;, thus bringing both these pieces of legislation on a par with each other in this respect. With these amendments and with the Supreme Court considering child abuse &ldquo;intolerable&rdquo;, there seems to be reasonable hope now that vulnerable children could be safer. The Bill is a step forward in preventing child abuse but the consequences of providing for the death penalty need to be closely observed.</strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\">&nbsp;</p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Q9. Which one of the following statements is not true about POSCO Bill according to the above paragraph?</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"isComprehension\": false,\n          \"paragraph\": null,\n          \"paragraphText\": null,\n          \"fillUpsAnswers\": [],\n          \"nameText\": \"Directions (6-10): Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.\\nThere has been much development recently with respect to the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012. With an objective of stopping the rampant sexual abuse of children, the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (Amendment) Bill, 2019 was introduced in the Rajya Sabha in July, and later passed by both Houses of Parliament. It is all set to become the ‘law of the land’. The present bill is welcome in certain respects as it specifically defines what ‘child pornography’ is; ‘using a child for pornographic purposes’ and for ‘possessing or storing pornography involving a child’ is punishable. It has also widened the ambit of ‘Aggravated sexual assault’.\\n\\nThe highlight of the Bill is the introduction of the death penalty for the rape of minors. The Bill, in its object clause, justifies this by referring to the judgments of the Supreme Court in Machhi Singh (1983) and Devender Pal Singh (2002) in which the court has held that the death penalty can be awarded only in rarest of rare cases. Thus the intention of the Bill is to have a deterrent effect; but it can be argued that the introduction of the death penalty may backfire in cases of child sexual abuse and even have a catastrophic effect. Often, the perpetrators of abuse are family members and having such penalty in the statute book may discourage the registration of the crime itself. Also, it may threaten the life of the minor as the maximum punishment for murder is also the death sentence.\\n \\nThe Justice J.S. Verma Committee, which was constituted in 2013 in the aftermath of the Nirbhaya case, after due deliberations found itself against the imposition of death penalty in rape cases. The 262nd Report of the Law Commission of India, 2015, also provides for abolition of the death penalty except in terror cases.\\nToday, the death penalty has become a prominent tool of symbolic legislation — a political statement indeed. Many a time, the Government, by introducing the death penalty, portrays itself to be strict and serious with regard to such offences. It largely diverts attention from the core issues of infrastructural apathy, procedural lapses and trial delays and conveniently evades the fact that ‘it is the certainty of punishment rather than its severity which has deterrence in real sense’. It is pertinent to note here that even a year-and-a-half after the passage of the Criminal Law (Amendment) Bill, 2018, which introduced the death penalty for rape of a minor girl, such incidents have not been under check. The debate here is not about retaining or abolishing the death penalty but the probable _________________________(A) of its provision in the Act.\\n\\nThe Supreme Court has recently taken cognisance of the sexual abuse of children, directing its registry to file a case as writ petition with cause title “In-re Alarming Rise in The Number of Reported Child Rape Incidents”. The court has also observed that it intends having a ‘zero tolerance policy’ toward child rape. As data on sexual crimes against children collected by the court show, 24,212 FIRs were filed across India from January to June this year. According to National Crime Records Bureau data of 2016, the conviction rate in POCSO cases is 29.6% while pendency is as high as 89%. The prescribed time period of two months for trial in such cases is hardly complied with.\\n \\nThe court has also taken note of the delay in trials, in turn directing the Central Government to set up special courts within 60 days of the order in each district having more than 100 pending cases under the Act. It is to be seen how long it takes to comply with the order.\\nThe Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2018 introduced the death penalty for rape of girls below the age of 12. At the same time, the POCSO Act, under Section 42, provides that where the same act constitutes an offence under the said Act and any other law, then the offender will be punished under the Act or such law, whichever provides for greater punishment. This has created an issue as the effect of such an amendment was death penalty for rape of minor girls but not for assault against minor boys.\\n \\n\\nThe proposed Bill does away with such a discrepancy. It is gender neutral and provides for the death penalty for “aggravated penetrative sexual assault of a child”, thus bringing both these pieces of legislation on a par with each other in this respect. With these amendments and with the Supreme Court considering child abuse “intolerable”, there seems to be reasonable hope now that vulnerable children could be safer. The Bill is a step forward in preventing child abuse but the consequences of providing for the death penalty need to be closely observed.\\n \\nQ9. Which one of the following statements is not true about POSCO Bill according to the above paragraph?\",\n          \"sectionId\": \"60f69fb8e70c56aee9070d22\",\n          \"order\": 9,\n          \"questionSubmitOrder\": 9,\n          \"options\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Amendment Bill, 2019 was introduced in July in Rajya Sabha.</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Amendment Bill, 2019 was introduced in July in Rajya Sabha.\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa4671651df7\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Introduction of Death penalty may fall back in case of child abuse</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Introduction of Death penalty may fall back in case of child abuse\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aabb70651df8\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The court has held the death penalty for rarest of rare cases.</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"The court has held the death penalty for rarest of rare cases.\",\n              \"isCorrect\": true,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa6b93651df9\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">It intends to have a zero tolerance policy towards child rape cases</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"It intends to have a zero tolerance policy towards child rape cases\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aac0ad651dfa\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">None of the above</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"None of the above\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa0e50651dfb\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"solution\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></strong></p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">Statement given in option (c) is not true because death penalty it is introduced in minor rape cases. Thus, option (c) is the right answer choice.</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"hasMultipleAnswers\": false,\n          \"type\": \"multiple_choice\",\n          \"isAttempted\": false,\n          \"isPartiallyCorrect\": false,\n          \"isCorrect\": false,\n          \"isGraceMarked\": false,\n          \"isReevaluated\": false,\n          \"isBookmark\": false,\n          \"studentQuestionId\": \"6ab02797e59d37f9583ba721\",\n          \"marks\": {\n            \"positive\": 0,\n            \"negative\": 0\n          },\n          \"timeTaken\": 0,\n          \"markForReview\": false,\n          \"isGpscType\": false,\n          \"isSurrendered\": false,\n          \"difficultyLevel\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"_id\": \"60f69fd0104d4397f076b0d9\",\n          \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Directions (6-10):&nbsp;Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">There has been much development recently with respect to the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012. With an objective of stopping the rampant sexual abuse of children, the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (Amendment) Bill, 2019 was introduced in the Rajya Sabha in July, and later passed by both Houses of Parliament. It is all set to become the &lsquo;law of the land&rsquo;. The present bill is welcome in certain respects as it specifically defines what &lsquo;child pornography&rsquo; is; &lsquo;using a child for pornographic purposes&rsquo; and for &lsquo;possessing or storing pornography involving a child&rsquo; is punishable. It has also widened the ambit of &lsquo;Aggravated sexual assault&rsquo;.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The highlight of the Bill is the introduction of the death penalty for the rape of minors. The Bill, in its object clause, justifies this by referring to the judgments of the Supreme Court in Machhi Singh (1983) and Devender Pal Singh (2002) in which the court has held that the death penalty can be awarded only in rarest of rare cases. Thus the intention of the Bill is to have a deterrent effect; but it can be argued that the introduction of the death penalty may backfire in cases of child sexual abuse and even have a catastrophic effect. Often, the perpetrators of abuse are family members and having such penalty in the statute book may discourage the registration of the crime itself. Also, it may threaten the life of the minor as the maximum punishment for murder is also the death sentence.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The Justice J.S. Verma Committee, which was constituted in 2013 in the aftermath of the Nirbhaya case, after due deliberations found itself against the imposition of death penalty in rape cases. The 262nd Report of the Law Commission of India, 2015, also provides for abolition of the death penalty except in terror cases.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><em><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Today, the death penalty has become a prominent tool of symbolic legislation</span></em></strong><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;&mdash; a political statement indeed. Many a time, the Government, by introducing the death penalty, portrays itself to be strict and serious with regard to such offences. It largely diverts attention from the core issues of infrastructural apathy, procedural lapses and trial delays and conveniently evades the fact that &lsquo;it is the certainty of punishment rather than its severity which has deterrence in real sense&rsquo;. It is pertinent to note here that even a year-and-a-half after the passage of the Criminal Law (Amendment) Bill, 2018, which introduced the death penalty for rape of a minor girl, such incidents have not been under check. The debate here is not about retaining or abolishing the death penalty but the probable _________________________(A) of its provision in the Act.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The Supreme Court has recently taken cognisance of the sexual abuse of children, directing its registry to file a case as writ petition with cause title &ldquo;In-re Alarming Rise in The Number of Reported Child Rape Incidents&rdquo;. The court has also observed that it intends having a &lsquo;zero tolerance policy&rsquo; toward child rape. As data on sexual crimes against children collected by the court show, 24,212 FIRs were filed across India from January to June this year. According to National Crime Records Bureau data of 2016, the conviction rate in POCSO cases is 29.6% while pendency is as high as 89%. The prescribed time period of two months for trial in such cases is hardly complied with.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The court has also taken note of the delay in trials, in turn directing the Central Government to set up special courts within 60 days of the order in each district having more than 100 pending cases under the Act. It is to be seen how long it takes to comply with the order.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2018 introduced the death penalty for rape of girls below the age of 12. At the same time, the POCSO Act, under Section 42, provides that where the same act constitutes an offence under the said Act and any other law, then the offender will be punished under the Act or such law, whichever provides for greater punishment. This has created an issue as the effect of such an amendment was death penalty for rape of minor girls but not for assault against minor boys.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /></span></strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /></span></p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 12pt; background-color: #f9f9f9; text-align: justify;\\\">The proposed Bill does away with such a discrepancy. It is gender neutral and provides for the death penalty for &ldquo;aggravated penetrative sexual assault of a child&rdquo;, thus bringing both these pieces of legislation on a par with each other in this respect. With these amendments and with the Supreme Court considering child abuse &ldquo;intolerable&rdquo;, there seems to be reasonable hope now that vulnerable children could be safer. The Bill is a step forward in preventing child abuse but the consequences of providing for the death penalty need to be closely observed.</strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\">&nbsp;</p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Q10. Choose among the following most suitable word to fill the blank given in (A)</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"isComprehension\": false,\n          \"paragraph\": null,\n          \"paragraphText\": null,\n          \"fillUpsAnswers\": [],\n          \"nameText\": \"Directions (6-10): Read the given passage and answer the following questions based on the passage.\\nThere has been much development recently with respect to the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012. With an objective of stopping the rampant sexual abuse of children, the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (Amendment) Bill, 2019 was introduced in the Rajya Sabha in July, and later passed by both Houses of Parliament. It is all set to become the ‘law of the land’. The present bill is welcome in certain respects as it specifically defines what ‘child pornography’ is; ‘using a child for pornographic purposes’ and for ‘possessing or storing pornography involving a child’ is punishable. It has also widened the ambit of ‘Aggravated sexual assault’.\\n\\nThe highlight of the Bill is the introduction of the death penalty for the rape of minors. The Bill, in its object clause, justifies this by referring to the judgments of the Supreme Court in Machhi Singh (1983) and Devender Pal Singh (2002) in which the court has held that the death penalty can be awarded only in rarest of rare cases. Thus the intention of the Bill is to have a deterrent effect; but it can be argued that the introduction of the death penalty may backfire in cases of child sexual abuse and even have a catastrophic effect. Often, the perpetrators of abuse are family members and having such penalty in the statute book may discourage the registration of the crime itself. Also, it may threaten the life of the minor as the maximum punishment for murder is also the death sentence.\\n \\nThe Justice J.S. Verma Committee, which was constituted in 2013 in the aftermath of the Nirbhaya case, after due deliberations found itself against the imposition of death penalty in rape cases. The 262nd Report of the Law Commission of India, 2015, also provides for abolition of the death penalty except in terror cases.\\nToday, the death penalty has become a prominent tool of symbolic legislation — a political statement indeed. Many a time, the Government, by introducing the death penalty, portrays itself to be strict and serious with regard to such offences. It largely diverts attention from the core issues of infrastructural apathy, procedural lapses and trial delays and conveniently evades the fact that ‘it is the certainty of punishment rather than its severity which has deterrence in real sense’. It is pertinent to note here that even a year-and-a-half after the passage of the Criminal Law (Amendment) Bill, 2018, which introduced the death penalty for rape of a minor girl, such incidents have not been under check. The debate here is not about retaining or abolishing the death penalty but the probable _________________________(A) of its provision in the Act.\\n\\nThe Supreme Court has recently taken cognisance of the sexual abuse of children, directing its registry to file a case as writ petition with cause title “In-re Alarming Rise in The Number of Reported Child Rape Incidents”. The court has also observed that it intends having a ‘zero tolerance policy’ toward child rape. As data on sexual crimes against children collected by the court show, 24,212 FIRs were filed across India from January to June this year. According to National Crime Records Bureau data of 2016, the conviction rate in POCSO cases is 29.6% while pendency is as high as 89%. The prescribed time period of two months for trial in such cases is hardly complied with.\\n \\nThe court has also taken note of the delay in trials, in turn directing the Central Government to set up special courts within 60 days of the order in each district having more than 100 pending cases under the Act. It is to be seen how long it takes to comply with the order.\\nThe Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2018 introduced the death penalty for rape of girls below the age of 12. At the same time, the POCSO Act, under Section 42, provides that where the same act constitutes an offence under the said Act and any other law, then the offender will be punished under the Act or such law, whichever provides for greater punishment. This has created an issue as the effect of such an amendment was death penalty for rape of minor girls but not for assault against minor boys.\\n \\n\\nThe proposed Bill does away with such a discrepancy. It is gender neutral and provides for the death penalty for “aggravated penetrative sexual assault of a child”, thus bringing both these pieces of legislation on a par with each other in this respect. With these amendments and with the Supreme Court considering child abuse “intolerable”, there seems to be reasonable hope now that vulnerable children could be safer. The Bill is a step forward in preventing child abuse but the consequences of providing for the death penalty need to be closely observed.\\n \\nQ10. Choose among the following most suitable word to fill the blank given in (A)\",\n          \"sectionId\": \"60f69fb8e70c56aee9070d22\",\n          \"order\": 10,\n          \"questionSubmitOrder\": 10,\n          \"options\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Acumen</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Acumen\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa07d5651e06\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Multifarious</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Multifarious\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa21b4651e07\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Ramifications</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Ramifications\",\n              \"isCorrect\": true,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa0393651e08\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Exacerbate</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Exacerbate\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa54bf651e09\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Phlegmatic</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Phlegmatic\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa655d651e0a\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"solution\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">Ramification- Complex or unwelcomed consequences of an action of event</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">(a)Acumen-Ability to make good judgments and make quick decisions</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">(b)Multifarious-Of various types</span></strong></p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">(c)Exacerbate- Making worse, having an unemotional and calm disposition</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"hasMultipleAnswers\": false,\n          \"type\": \"multiple_choice\",\n          \"isAttempted\": false,\n          \"isPartiallyCorrect\": false,\n          \"isCorrect\": false,\n          \"isGraceMarked\": false,\n          \"isReevaluated\": false,\n          \"isBookmark\": false,\n          \"studentQuestionId\": \"6ab02797e59d3722153ba722\",\n          \"marks\": {\n            \"positive\": 0,\n            \"negative\": 0\n          },\n          \"timeTaken\": 0,\n          \"markForReview\": false,\n          \"isGpscType\": false,\n          \"isSurrendered\": false,\n          \"difficultyLevel\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"_id\": \"60f69fd0104d4335f676b0de\",\n          \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Directions (11-15):&nbsp;Read the following passage and answer the following questions based on the given passage.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">As the country celebrates 50 years since bank nationalisation, bank employees will be proud that they have contributed to nation-building. Still there are basic issues close to the bankman&rsquo;s heart that are yet to find redressal, observes Jyothi KP, Vice-President, All-India Bank Officers&rsquo; Confederation, Kerala, and a National Executive Committee member.The Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertakings) Acts of 1970 and 1980 provide that the Central Government, in consultation with the Reserve Bank of India, may make a scheme, inter alia, for amalgamation of any nationalised bank with any other nationalised bank or any other banking institution. Various committees, including Narasimhan Committee (1998) constituted by the RBI, Leeladhar Committee (2008) chaired by the RBI Deputy Governor, and the Nayak Committee (2014) constituted by the RBI, have recommended consolidation of public sector banks (PSBs) given&nbsp;underlying benefits/synergies.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Taking note of this and potential benefits of consolidation for banks as well as the public through enhanced access to banking services, the government, in order to facilitate consolidation among PSBs to create strong and competitive banks, serving as catalysts for growth, with improved risk profile, has approved a framework for proposals to amalgamate PSBs through an Alternative Mechanism.The following amalgamation/mergers have already happened post-nationalisation and some more are reportedly in the pipeline. This creates stress for the employees since they are scared for their existence and job security, observes Jyothi.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /></span></strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Giving a bank employee&rsquo;s perspective, Jyothi recalled that the banking sector was in the red in Q4 of 2018-19 with a loss of ₹20,817 crore. In 2017-18, they had reported a massive loss of ₹85,000 crore forcing lenders to make provisions for bad loans and clean-up of the books.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Till such time a project is commissioned in line with the approved schedule, banks should not be forced to categorise such projects as NPA, notes Jyothi. In the event of classification of big-ticket advances as NPA, the government should pro-actively help banks in realising the public money. It should initiate steps to declare suo moto 50 top NPAs of each banks as wilful defaulters and to publish their names and photos.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">For implementing SARFAESI provisions, many bank officers are targeted, even personally. By merely targeting the bank officers responsible for executing the Act, the political fraternity is missing the point to bring amendments to the Act itself.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The humongous challenge of realising the NPA is telling on PSB bottomlines. This is also subjecting the desk officers to unreasonable amount of work pressure and stress.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Irregular working hours, stress on account of financial risk in routine work schedules, technical problems of software, and IT-related issues add to the worries of the already traumatised bank employees.In many cases, their retirement benefits are withheld. Many are arrested and are not aware as to why the arrest is happening in the first place, evening leading to suicides.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">According to Section 5 of the Banking Regulation Act, banking means &lsquo;accepting, for the purpose of lending and investment of deposits of money from public, repayable on demand or otherwise and withdrawal by cheque, draft, order or otherwise.&rsquo;However, sale of para-banking products and implementation of government schemes take away the lion&rsquo;s share of working hours of an average PSB employee.As mentioned earlier, when the balance sheet turns red, all blame is shoved on the desk-level officers for non-profitability/non- performance.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /></span></strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\"><br /><br /><!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]--><br /><!--[endif]--></span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Yet, when it comes to wage revision, the question of profitability arise. The settlement takes place between Indian Banks&rsquo; Association (IBA) and United Forum of Bank Unions (UFBU).</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: justify; line-height: 21.75pt; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">&nbsp;</span></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The IBA represents bank managements and the UFBU, officers, employees, and staff organisations. The Charter of demands had been submitted in a time bound manner and the IBA had agreed to complete the process of negotiations expeditiously within the time-frame so as to reach the settlement before October 2017. However, since five major bank managements have not given full mandate for negotiating wages up to scale VII , the AIBOC is not participating in the talks now.</span></strong></p>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Q11. Why amalgamation of Public sector banks has become a stress lately for the bank employees?</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"isComprehension\": false,\n          \"paragraph\": null,\n          \"paragraphText\": null,\n          \"fillUpsAnswers\": [],\n          \"nameText\": \"Directions (11-15): Read the following passage and answer the following questions based on the given passage.\\n\\nAs the country celebrates 50 years since bank nationalisation, bank employees will be proud that they have contributed to nation-building. Still there are basic issues close to the bankman’s heart that are yet to find redressal, observes Jyothi KP, Vice-President, All-India Bank Officers’ Confederation, Kerala, and a National Executive Committee member.The Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertakings) Acts of 1970 and 1980 provide that the Central Government, in consultation with the Reserve Bank of India, may make a scheme, inter alia, for amalgamation of any nationalised bank with any other nationalised bank or any other banking institution. Various committees, including Narasimhan Committee (1998) constituted by the RBI, Leeladhar Committee (2008) chaired by the RBI Deputy Governor, and the Nayak Committee (2014) constituted by the RBI, have recommended consolidation of public sector banks (PSBs) given underlying benefits/synergies.\\nTaking note of this and potential benefits of consolidation for banks as well as the public through enhanced access to banking services, the government, in order to facilitate consolidation among PSBs to create strong and competitive banks, serving as catalysts for growth, with improved risk profile, has approved a framework for proposals to amalgamate PSBs through an Alternative Mechanism.The following amalgamation/mergers have already happened post-nationalisation and some more are reportedly in the pipeline. This creates stress for the employees since they are scared for their existence and job security, observes Jyothi.\\n\\nGiving a bank employee’s perspective, Jyothi recalled that the banking sector was in the red in Q4 of 2018-19 with a loss of ₹20,817 crore. In 2017-18, they had reported a massive loss of ₹85,000 crore forcing lenders to make provisions for bad loans and clean-up of the books.\\n \\nTill such time a project is commissioned in line with the approved schedule, banks should not be forced to categorise such projects as NPA, notes Jyothi. In the event of classification of big-ticket advances as NPA, the government should pro-actively help banks in realising the public money. It should initiate steps to declare suo moto 50 top NPAs of each banks as wilful defaulters and to publish their names and photos.\\nFor implementing SARFAESI provisions, many bank officers are targeted, even personally. By merely targeting the bank officers responsible for executing the Act, the political fraternity is missing the point to bring amendments to the Act itself.\\n\\nThe humongous challenge of realising the NPA is telling on PSB bottomlines. This is also subjecting the desk officers to unreasonable amount of work pressure and stress.\\n \\nIrregular working hours, stress on account of financial risk in routine work schedules, technical problems of software, and IT-related issues add to the worries of the already traumatised bank employees.In many cases, their retirement benefits are withheld. Many are arrested and are not aware as to why the arrest is happening in the first place, evening leading to suicides.\\nAccording to Section 5 of the Banking Regulation Act, banking means ‘accepting, for the purpose of lending and investment of deposits of money from public, repayable on demand or otherwise and withdrawal by cheque, draft, order or otherwise.’However, sale of para-banking products and implementation of government schemes take away the lion’s share of working hours of an average PSB employee.As mentioned earlier, when the balance sheet turns red, all blame is shoved on the desk-level officers for non-profitability/non- performance.\\n\\nYet, when it comes to wage revision, the question of profitability arise. The settlement takes place between Indian Banks’ Association (IBA) and United Forum of Bank Unions (UFBU).\\n \\nThe IBA represents bank managements and the UFBU, officers, employees, and staff organisations. The Charter of demands had been submitted in a time bound manner and the IBA had agreed to complete the process of negotiations expeditiously within the time-frame so as to reach the settlement before October 2017. However, since five major bank managements have not given full mandate for negotiating wages up to scale VII , the AIBOC is not participating in the talks now.\\n \\nQ11. Why amalgamation of Public sector banks has become a stress lately for the bank employees?\",\n          \"sectionId\": \"60f69fb8e70c56aee9070d22\",\n          \"order\": 11,\n          \"questionSubmitOrder\": 11,\n          \"options\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;Proposals to amalgamate PSBs via an alternative method is approved by the government</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \" Proposals to amalgamate PSBs via an alternative method is approved by the government\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa4dac651e29\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">The job security and existence of a bank employees becomes questionable</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"The job security and existence of a bank employees becomes questionable\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aac864651e2a\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Because such amalgamations have been done after nationalisation and still followed by more such mergers.</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"Because such amalgamations have been done after nationalisation and still followed by more such mergers.\",\n              \"isCorrect\": true,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aab389651e2b\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #F9F9F9;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">&nbsp;During the year 2018-2019, banking was insolvent with a loss of 20,817 Crore Rs.</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \" During the year 2018-2019, banking was insolvent with a loss of 20,817 Crore Rs.\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa4b83651e2c\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"<p><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">All of the above</span></p>\",\n              \"nameText\": \"All of the above\",\n              \"isCorrect\": false,\n              \"_id\": \"60f6b26d3be0aa1480651e2d\",\n              \"isMarked\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"solution\": \"<p><strong style=\\\"text-align: justify; font-size: 1.4rem;\\\"><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: 'Arial','sans-serif'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: blue; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\\\">As it is mentioned in paragraph second, due to amalgamation of banks, the job security and existence of employees of the bank being amalgamated creates stress as their existence becomes questionable. Hence, option (b) is correct.</span></strong></p>\",\n          \"hasMultipleAnswers\": false,\n          \"type\": \"multiple_choice\",\n          \"isAttempted\": false,\n          \"isPartiallyCorrect\": false,\n          \"isCorrect\": false,\n          \"isGraceMarked\": false,\n          \"isReevaluated\": false,\n          \"isBookmark\": false,\n          \"studentQuestionId\": \"6ab02797e59d3756fe3ba723\",\n          \"marks\": {\n            \"positive\": 0,\n            \"negative\": 0\n          },\n          \"timeTaken\": 0,\n          \"markForReview\": false,\n          \"isGpscType\": false,\n          \"isSurrendered\": false,\n          \"difficultyLevel\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"_id\": \"60f69fd0104d4304b276b0e3\",\n          \"name\": \"<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65); text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">Directions (11-15):&nbsp;Read the following passage and answer the following questions based on the given passage.</span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65); text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\"><br /><br /></span></strong></p>\\n<p class=\\\"MsoNormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65); text-align: justify; line-height: normal; background: #f9f9f9;\\\"><strong><span lang=\\\"EN-IN\\\" style=\\\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\\">As the country celebrates 50 years since bank nationalisation, bank employees will be proud that they have contributed to nation-building. Still there are basic issues close to the bankman&rsquo;s heart that are 